Context: in his 1983 Teichmüller notes Grothendieck works with a category S_*C̃D^±_top. Its objects are triples (X, Σ̃, R): a compact topological surface X, a 'cut system' Σ̃ (a disjoint union of circles containing ∂X), and a finite set R of marked points on Σ̃. The previous page defines a functor Γ from this category to 'MD graphs' (S, A⃗, σ, ·, γ, ρ):
- S = π_0(X∖Σ̃), the faces;
- A⃗ = pairs (circle, side), the half-edges;
- σ = the involution exchanging sides, fixing boundary circles;
- each half-edge goes to its incident face;
- γ: S→N gives the genus of each piece;
- ρ: A=π_0(Σ̃)→N gives card(R∩C).
That page also claims Γ is a '0-isomorphism': bijective on π_0 and surjective on π_1.

This page:
(1) Γ commutes with the 'connected components' operation, which he calls the most trivial of the operations to be transcribed combinatorially. Hence X is connected iff Γ(X) is connected, and more precisely π_0(X) ≅ π_0(Γ(X)).
(2) A margin note poses the task of computing the genus of each component α∈π_0(X)≅π_0(Γ) from the combinatorics. It answers: g = Σ_{s∈S} g_s + c(Γ), where c(Γ) is the cyclomatic number rank H^1(Γ). This is the usual genus formula for a surface glued from pieces along a graph, as for the dual graph of a stable curve.
(3) Special case a): Σ=∅, Σ̃=∂X, i.e. the cut system is only the boundary. Then every edge is free (σ = id, A = A⃗ = A⃗^+), so there are no bound edges and all vertices are isolated. The graph reduces to the data A →(r) N, A → S, S →(γ) N: a disjoint union of decorated vertices with legs. The margin figure shows vertices labelled g_0,…,g_4, each with legs.
(4) The connected case T^±_top, taking ρ=0, is card S = 1. The surface is then classified by the pair (g, ν), with g=γ(s) the genus and ν = card A the number of holes. This is the classical classification of surfaces with boundary.

The page serves the programme of translating topological operations on decomposed surfaces (the Teichmüller tower / Lego of the Esquisse) into the combinatorics of decorated graphs.
